  "body": "For the longest time I was under the impression that the definitions of length for Javascript strings and for (not explicitly Javascript) strings were equivalent. The difference is easy to miss when skimming through the spec, but what makes it clear that the definition can be confusing is that the code unit prefix algorithm just below uses the wrong kind of length:\r\n\r\n> Let `aCodeUnit` be the `i`th [code unit](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#code-unit) of `a` if `i` is less than `a`’s [length](https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-length); otherwise null.\r\n\r\n\"Length\" there links to the definition of a (not explicitly Javascript) string's length, which measures code points, not code units. Aside from changing the link, the text would also need to be updated, though I'm not sure if something like \"`a`'s length as a Javascript string\" would be the way to do it.\r\n\r\nI'd also ask for a note clarifying the difference between the lengths, but that can be its own separate issue if need be.",
